{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Elohim spoke all these words.</b> As it were, like a person who arranges matters in his mind in order to say them to others — so 'and He spoke' between Himself and Himself, and afterward to say them to Israel: 'I am YHWH your Elohim.' As it is written: 'Then He saw and recounted it; He established it, and also searched it out, and He said to man' etc. — He taught you the way of the world: if you are a Torah scholar, do not let your spirit be arrogant over you, to say a matter before the public until you work it through between yourself and yourself two or three times. A story of Rabbi Akiva: the sexton called him in the synagogue to read from the Torah, and he did not want to go up. His students said to him: Rabbi, did you not teach us thus — 'for it is your life and the length of your days' — and why did you refrain from going up? He said to them: I vow, I did not refrain from going up, but because I had not arranged the portion between myself and myself two or three times, for a person is not permitted to say a matter before the public until he works it through between himself and himself two or three times." "<b>All these words.</b> Rashi explained: this teaches that HaKadosh Baruch Hu said all the commandments in one utterance — something impossible for a person to say. If so, what does Scripture still mean to say, 'I am' and 'You shall have no'? It teaches that He went back and explained each and every utterance separately. Explanation: if HaKadosh Baruch Hu said all Ten Commandments, what does Scripture still mean to say, 'I am' and 'You shall have no' — that is, why were they repeated? And he answers: it teaches that He went back and explained each and every utterance etc. — that is, at first HaKadosh Baruch Hu said them all at once and in one utterance, and Israel did not understand them; and the fact that they were repeated teaches that the Speaker went back and explained each and every utterance separately, so that Israel would understand them. 'I am' and 'You shall have no' — HaKadosh Baruch Hu went back and explained, and they did not have the capacity to bear and to hear from His mouth any more, as it is written: 'You speak with us and we will hear, and let not Elohim speak with us lest we die.' And so our Rabbis said: 'I am' and 'You shall have no' — we heard them from the mouth of the Almighty; Moses came and went back and explained from there onward each and every utterance separately. And this appears correct, for the style of the utterances 'I am' and 'You shall have no' sounds like a person speaking about himself, while the rest sounds like a person speaking about others. As it is written: 'You shall not take the name of YHWH your Elohim' — and it is not written 'My name.' 'For six days YHWH made' — and it is not written 'I made,' and so too the rest. And what Rashi explained — 'it teaches that He went back and explained' — refers both to HaKadosh Baruch Hu and to Moses: each one went back and explained his portion of the commandments, as explained."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:02.037212297Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}